course: dictionary
lesson: 3117
title: Electoral: words about voting
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Electoral» can look hard, but the word around it gives a clue. You may already know «election», when people vote to choose leaders. Electoral means to do with elections.
explain: Look at the phrase «electoral roll». A roll is a list, so the electoral roll is the list of people allowed to vote. Try it in a sentence: «Your name must be on the electoral roll before you can vote.» It makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Context also helps with bigger phrases. «The Electoral College» is a system for choosing a US president. Once you know electoral means to do with elections, the whole phrase becomes clearer.
fact: Electoral means to do with elections, where people vote to choose leaders or representatives.
fact: «Electoral roll» means the list of people allowed to vote.
fact: «Electoral College» is the US system for choosing a president.
fact: A candidate needs 270 of the 538 Electoral College votes to become US president.
fact: «Electoral fraud» means cheating in elections.
exercise: «Your name must be on the electoral roll before you can vote.» What is the electoral roll? | A map of the country's towns | *The list of people allowed to vote | A speech made by the leader | A prize given to the winning candidate
exercise: What does electoral mean? | To do with electricity and power cables | Very old and full of history | *To do with elections, where people vote | Sad and quiet
exercise: «A candidate needs 270 of the 538 Electoral College votes to become US president.» How many votes does a candidate need? | 538 | 135 | *270 | 300
test: «Electoral fraud» is a serious crime. What does it mean? | *Cheating in elections | Painting election posters | A holiday for voters | Counting the trees in a park
test: Which phrase names the list of people allowed to vote? | Electoral College | *electoral roll | electoral fraud
exam: In the US, the Electoral College chooses the president. What is the Electoral College? | A map of the US states | *A system for choosing a US president | A group of teachers in a school | A list of prices
